  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="bl-art">
      <p class="lede">
        A technical hire now takes 17.6 interviews. In 2021 it took 11.6.<sup class="fn"
          ><a class="fn-r" href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">1</a></sup
        > Nobody decided to add six interviews to every hire. They arrived one round at a time, each for a sensible reason,
        and nobody took one away.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’ve run hiring for twenty years, for teams of five and for organisations of thousands. The pattern holds at
        every size. When a team isn’t sure what it’s looking for, it interviews more and hopes the extra rounds will
        make it sure. They rarely do.
      </p>
      <aside class="bl-mn">
        <span class="ql ql--i">Note</span>Ashby counts 17.6 interviews per technical hire in Q1 2026, against 11.6 in
        2021.
      </aside>
      <h2>Count the hours</h2>
      <p>
        Give each of those 17.6 interviews an hour, and leave the preparation and the write-up out of it. That is still
        more than seventeen hours of your senior people’s time for a single hire. On the other side of the desk, a
        recruiter closes about 3.8 technical hires a quarter.<sup class="fn"
          ><a class="fn-r" href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">1</a></sup
        >
      </p>
