      <p>
        Those hours don’t come from nowhere. They come out of the roadmap, out of code review, out of the one-to-ones
        your engineering managers keep cancelling. Hiring has quietly become one of the most expensive things your best
        people do, and almost nobody budgets for it.
      </p>
      <aside class="bl-mn">
        <span class="ql ql--i">Note</span>Each of the four has a fix in the process. None of them needs better
        interviewers.
      </aside>
      <h2>Where they go</h2>
      <p>
        When I sit with a team and walk through a stalled search, the extra interviews land in the same four places.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Nobody wrote the bar down.</b> Each interviewer carries their own picture of the role, so the candidate is assessed
        against four different jobs. A fifth round gets added to settle the disagreement. It settles nothing, because the
        disagreement was never about the candidate.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>The wrong people reach round two.</b> A thirty-minute screen checks the CV against the job description. It can’t
        tell whether the person has done the work, so the expensive interviews, the ones with your senior engineers, are spent
        finding out.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Feedback arrives late.</b> A scorecard written three days later records an impression. The evidence has already
        gone. Meanwhile the strong candidate accepts another offer, and the search restarts with whoever is still available.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>The last round repeats the first.</b> The final panel asks what the first panel asked, because nothing that was
        learned got passed along.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="pull">
        Write the bar first.
        <em>Every interview after it gets shorter.</em>
      </blockquote>
      <aside class="bl-mn">
        <span class="ql ql--i">Note</span>The intake is one call of about 45 minutes, once per role.
      </aside>
      <h2>What we do instead</h2>
      <p>This is the problem Tribera was built around, so I’ll be specific about what we change.</p>
      <p>
        We write the bar with your hiring manager before the first CV, in a call of about forty-five minutes. Then
        someone who has done the job runs the first serious conversation: thirty to forty-five minutes with each
        candidate, scored against that bar and recorded with consent. Your team meets the three who clear it, with the
        evidence in hand, and spends its own interviews on the questions that are still open.
      </p>
      <p>
        In one search for a consumer internet company in Bengaluru, two backend seats had been open for nine months. We
        screened 126 people. The team met five, and hired two.
      </p>
      <h2>If you change one thing</h2>
      <p>
        Before your next role opens, put the hiring manager and one interviewer in a room for forty-five minutes and
        write down what a yes looks like. Weight the skills. Agree what you won’t compromise on. Then count the
        interviews on that hire, and on the one after it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’d be surprised if the number doesn’t fall. I’d be more surprised if anyone on your team misses the rounds you
        stopped running.
      </p>

